  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 238 AM EDT FRI OCT 2 2026\n\nThe long term period will open with broad troughing over the eastern \nCONUS, with the trough axis initially west of the forecast area in \nthe Midwest. Meanwhile, a midlevel high will be centered over the \nFour Corners region, with a fairly broad area of ridging over the \nwestern CONUS. At the surface, an area of low pressure will \ninitially sit due south of the forecast area in Tennessee; this low \nwill attempt to lift north towards the forecast area early Sunday \nmorning before an additional cold front pushes through the area by \nSunday afternoon. Rain will remain likely in southeastern Kentucky \nthroughout the day given closer proximity to the surface low, with \nlesser chances to the northwest. Thankfully, widespread cloud cover \nand a relatively cool and saturated air mass will keep instability \nvery low (with LREF mean surface based CAPE of 100 J/kg on Sunday \nafternoon), so rain rates with this activity should remain meager, \nmitigating any flash flood risk. \n\nBy Monday morning, the front will have departed our area, with \nnortherly to north northeasterly postfrontal winds ushering in a \ncooler and drier air mass. At the same time, midlevel ridging will \nexpand east, leaving eastern Kentucky in a region of northwest flow \naloft near the base of the original trough. This pattern will \npersist through Wednesday, with highs remaining quite cool \n(generally ranging from the upper 60s in the northern forecast area \nto low 70s in the south). It will feel dry early next week, too; \ndewpoints will largely be in the 40s across the forecast area \nthrough Tuesday, courtesy of continued northeasterly winds around a \nsurface high over the Great Lakes region. Each night, clear skies \nand light winds will allow for temperatures to cool significantly, \nparticularly in valleys. Raw NBM output is running noticeably cooler \nthan deterministic models, even in valley locations, although MOS \nguidance does suggest valleys could reach the low 40s overnight \nMonday and Tuesday. Adding uncertainty, deterministic guidance still \ndisagrees regarding the magnitude of the pressure gradient over our \nforecast area; a tighter pressure gradient, favored by the GFS, \nwould prevent full decoupling, keeping valleys warmer. Thus, there \nis less confidence in exact low temperatures, although confidence is \nhigh that overnight temperatures will be below average. \n\nBy Wednesday, the surface high will continue to shift east, with \nwinds becoming more westerly during the afternoon hours, allowing \ndewpoints to rise modestly into the 50s. Lows will be a bit warmer\nWednesday night, only falling into the mid 40s. Still, weather \nlooks to be sunny through the end of the period, with high \ntemperatures climbing a few degrees by Thursday.",
